# Page Component Structure Pattern

## Overview
DECM follows a clean separation between route pages and UI components using a two-layer architecture.

## Directory Structure

```
apps/web/src/
├── pages/                      # Route pages (Next.js/React Router pages)
│   ├── index.tsx              # Route: /
│   ├── signup/
│   │   └── index.tsx          # Route: /signup
│   └── oauth/
│       └── google/
│           └── index.tsx      # Route: /oauth/google
└── components/
    └── pages/                  # Page UI components
        ├── LandingPage/
        │   └── LandingPage.tsx
        ├── SignUp/
        │   └── SignUp.tsx
        └── GoogleOAuth/
            ├── GoogleOAuth.tsx
            └── useVerifyGoogleOAuth.tsx
```

## Pattern: Separation of Concerns

### 1. **Pages Directory** (`/pages/`)
Route files should be minimal and only handle:
- SEO metadata (using `FaviconHelmet` or `CustomHelmet`)
- Route-level data fetching setup
- Importing and rendering the corresponding page component

**Example:**
```tsx
import { FaviconHelmet } from '@/components/providers/helmets/FaviconHelmet';
import { LandingPage } from '@/components/pages/LandingPage/LandingPage';

const IndexPage = () => {
    return (
        <>
            <FaviconHelmet
                title="Home | DECM - Decentralized Event Management"
                description="Welcome to DECM - Web 3.0 platform"
            />
            <LandingPage />
        </>
    );
};

export default IndexPage;
```

### 2. **Components/Pages Directory** (`/components/pages/`)
Page components contain all UI logic and should:
- Handle all visual rendering
- Implement business logic and state management
- Use hooks for data fetching
- Include all interactions and animations
- Use Typography components for text
- Implement i18n translations

**Example:**
```tsx
import { Typography } from '@/components/typography/typography';
import { useTranslation } from 'react-i18next';

export const LandingPage = () => {
    const { t } = useTranslation();
    
    return (
        <div className="min-h-screen">
            <Typography variant="header" tag="h1">
                {t('home.hero.title')}
            </Typography>
            {/* All UI components here */}
        </div>
    );
};
```

## Folder Organization

### Page Component Folder Structure
Each page component should have its own folder:

```
components/pages/PageName/
├── PageName.tsx              # Main component
├── usePageHook.tsx          # Custom hooks (if needed)
├── PageSubComponent.tsx     # Sub-components (if needed)
└── index.ts                 # Optional barrel export
```

## Best Practices

### ✅ DO:
- Keep route files minimal (10-20 lines max)
- Place all UI in `components/pages/`
- Use consistent naming: `LandingPage.tsx`, `SignUp.tsx`
- Export page components as named exports
- Co-locate page-specific hooks with the component
- Use helmet components for SEO in route files

### ❌ DON'T:
- Put complex UI logic in `/pages/` route files
- Mix routing concerns with UI rendering
- Create deeply nested component structures
- Export page components as default from `/components/pages/`
- Hard-code text strings (use i18n instead)

## Migration Pattern

When migrating existing pages:

1. **Identify** the route file in `/pages/`
2. **Extract** all UI and business logic
3. **Create** a new component in `/components/pages/PageName/`
4. **Keep** only the helmet and component import in `/pages/`
5. **Move** all functionality to the page component

## Example: Complete Page Setup

**Route File:** `apps/web/src/pages/oauth/google/index.tsx`
```tsx
import { FaviconHelmet } from '@/components/providers/helmets/FaviconHelmet';
import { GoogleOAuth } from '@/components/pages/GoogleOAuth/GoogleOAuth';
import { useTranslation } from 'react-i18next';

const GoogleOAuthPage = () => {
    const { t } = useTranslation();
    
    return (
        <>
            <FaviconHelmet
                title={`${t('oauth.google.title')} | ${t('common.appName')}`}
                description={t('oauth.google.subtitle')}
            />
            <GoogleOAuth />
        </>
    );
};

export default GoogleOAuthPage;
```

**Page Component:** `apps/web/src/components/pages/GoogleOAuth/GoogleOAuth.tsx`
```tsx
import { Typography } from '@/components/typography/typography';
import { useTranslation } from 'react-i18next';

export const GoogleOAuth = () => {
    const { t } = useTranslation();

    return (
        <div className="min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center">
            <Typography variant="header" tag="h1">
                {t('oauth.google.title')}
            </Typography>
            {/* All UI implementation */}
        </div>
    );
};
```

## Benefits

1. **Maintainability** - Clear separation makes code easier to understand
2. **Reusability** - Page components can be reused in different routes
3. **Testability** - UI components can be tested independently of routing
4. **SEO** - Route files handle metadata separately from UI
5. **Performance** - Easier to implement code splitting and lazy loading

